# Dagric OS — Desktop (KDE Plasma, hand-picked)
#
# Deliberately NOT using task-kde-desktop or kde-standard metapackages.
# Those drag in games, KDE PIM, Akonadi databases, and dozens of apps
# nobody asked for. Every package below earns its place.

# Core Plasma shell
plasma-desktop
plasma-workspace
# The X11 session, and it is a repair, not a preference. plasma-workspace ships
# /usr/share/xsessions/plasmax11.desktop unconditionally, so SDDM has always
# offered a "Plasma (X11)" entry — but kwin-x11 is only a Recommends alternative
# and this build passes --apt-recommends false, so /usr/bin/kwin_x11 was never
# on the image. Picking that entry gave you a desktop with no window manager:
# no title bars, no way to move or close anything. The owner most likely to
# pick it is the one whose Wayland session is already misbehaving on an older
# NVIDIA card, which is the worst possible moment to hand them a broken screen.
# 1.7 MB buys a working fallback. (0520-display-defaults removes the session
# entry when this package is absent, so dropping this line is safe too — it
# just takes the rescue option away with it.)
kwin-x11
plasma-nm
plasma-pa
powerdevil
bluedevil
kscreen
systemsettings
breeze-gtk-theme
kde-config-gtk-style

# Login manager
sddm
sddm-theme-breeze

# Wayland/X portals (screen sharing, file pickers for Flatpak apps)
xdg-desktop-portal-kde

# Essential KDE applications — one tool per job, no overlap
dolphin
konsole
kate
# KDE's spell-check backend. apps.list.chroot deliberately ships five hunspell
# dictionaries so that "a German owner gets no red underline anywhere in
# Writer" — LibreOffice links hunspell directly and is fine. Everything KDE
# does not: Kate, KMail and every Plasma text field go through Sonnet, whose
# hunspell plugin lives in sonnet6-plugins, a Recommends. Dropped, so the
# dictionaries are on the image and nothing in KDE can reach them.
sonnet6-plugins
ark

# Ark handles RAR by shelling out to a command-line tool and ships no such tool
# itself, so a double-clicked .rar gives "no suitable plugin found" on a stock
# image. app-ark.html says unpacking RAR is fine. unar is the unencumbered
# reader (Debian keeps unrar-free/unrar-nonfree separate for licensing); it also
# restores .cbr comics in Okular, which is the same gap from the other side.
# 5.9 MB.
unar
gwenview
okular

# Okular ships TEN generators and epub is not one of them. Read out of the .deb:
# base okular has comicbook, dvi, fax, fb, ghostview, kimgio, mobi, poppler, txt
# and xps; djvu, epub, md and tiff are in this Suggests, which apt never
# installs. app-okular.html names ePub as the first thing it opens, and this OS
# is sold to libraries — where .epub and .djvu are most of the collection.
# Opening one today gives "no plugin for this mimetype". 974 KB.
okular-extra-backends
kde-spectacle
kwalletmanager

# THE WALLET PROMPT THE MANUAL PROMISES YOU WILL NEVER SEE.
#
# app-kwallet.html says the wallet is unlocked by your login password "so you
# never see it". That is only true with the PAM module, and the PAM module is a
# Recommends, which --apt-recommends false drops. Without it the first Wi-Fi
# password saved pops the wallet-creation wizard asking a non-technical owner to
# invent a second password, and then a "KDE Wallet Service wants to open the
# wallet" dialog on every single login afterwards, forever.
#
# THE NAME IS THE TRAP. This is Plasma 6, so the obvious guess is
# libpam-kwallet6 — that package does not exist. The Plasma 6 module is still
# published as libpam-kwallet5 and its version is 6.3.4. Checked with
# `apt-get install -s`, not with the name that reads correctly. 43 KB.
libpam-kwallet5
print-manager

# Software center (Flatpak-backed)
plasma-discover
plasma-discover-backend-flatpak
# Firmware updates (LVFS) in the GUI. fwupd itself is in system.list, but
# without this backend Discover shows no firmware at all and updates are
# CLI-only via fwupdmgr — which the Windows-switcher audience will never find.
plasma-discover-backend-fwupd

# Desktop completeness: network browsing in Dolphin, video/document
# thumbnails, a calculator, and a task-manager equivalent (Ctrl+Esc)
kio-extras
ffmpegthumbs
kdegraphics-thumbnailers
kcalc
plasma-systemmonitor

# Accessibility — a screen reader is a legal procurement requirement in many
# markets (EU EN 301 549, US Section 508); without it the OS can't be sold
# to institutions and is unusable for blind users. Orca is MUTE without a
# voice module, and exactly ONE of the three lines below is what keeps it
# talking. Naming which one matters, because the wrong reason gets "cleaned up"
# by the next person:
#
#   speech-dispatcher            hard Depends of orca. Never at risk.
#   speech-dispatcher-audio-plugins  hard Depends of speech-dispatcher
#                                (versioned =). Never at risk either; it is
#                                what provides spd_pipewire.so / spd_pulse.so.
#   speech-dispatcher-espeak-ng  ** THE LOAD-BEARING PIN. ** It is only a
#                                Recommends of speech-dispatcher, and
#                                auto/config builds --apt-recommends false, so
#                                without this line spd runs and says nothing.
#                                Verified absent in the built image once.
#   espeak-ng                    the CLI. NOT what makes Orca speak —
#                                speech-dispatcher-espeak-ng pulls
#                                libespeak-ng1 + espeak-ng-data itself. Kept
#                                because spd-say/espeak-ng is what a spoken
#                                boot prompt would use, and it costs little.
#
# 0530-accessibility.hook.chroot re-checks this whole chain on the built image
# and fails the build if any link is missing. Debian's orca package ships no
# .desktop file at all, so that hook also adds the menu entries — see it.
orca
speech-dispatcher-espeak-ng
espeak-ng

# Disk/power plumbing the desktop expects
udisks2
upower

# The QML runtime, for the Dagric Appearance gallery.
# The image ALREADY carries every QML module that gallery imports — QtQuick,
# QtQuick.Controls and QtQuick.Layouts all ship because Plasma itself is built
# on them. What was missing was the interpreter that runs a .qml file outside
# of Plasma: /usr/bin/qml6 simply did not exist, so a native gallery was
# impossible. This package is that interpreter and nothing more (about 150 kB);
# its dependencies — libqt6qml6, libqt6gui6, libqt6widgets6 — are already
# pulled in by plasma-desktop, so this adds no new libraries to the image.
qml-qt6

# SMART disk-failure warnings, KDE-native: a kded daemon that watches disk
# health and notifies the user in plain language when a drive degrades.
# Pairs with smartmontools (system list) which powers the Security Checkup.
plasma-disks

# "Run as administrator", which Windows has on every right-click and Dagric
# had nowhere. kio-admin gives Dolphin a real admin:// protocol behind a polkit
# prompt, so editing a system file is one context-menu entry.
#
# This is a SAFETY feature, not a convenience. Without it the internet teaches
# the folklore answer — `sudo dolphin` — which runs a whole GUI file manager as
# root, leaves root-owned files in the user's own config directory, and is the
# single most common way a new Linux user quietly breaks their desktop. Giving
# them the correct gesture is how you stop them learning the dangerous one.
kio-admin

# The two Windows utilities with no equivalent on an installed Dagric.
#
# partitionmanager is Disk Management: see the drives, format a USB stick,
# resize a partition, without a terminal. isoimagewriter is Rufus — the tool
# somebody needs to make ANOTHER Dagric stick, or a Windows recovery stick, and
# the one thing a person who has just switched is most likely to want to do for
# a friend. Both are KDE-native so they match the desktop rather than dragging
# in GTK.
#
# Named in plain English by dagric-app-names, with the Windows vocabulary
# (disk management, format, diskpart, rufus, balenaetcher) in Keywords so the
# launcher finds them from what the owner already calls these things.
partitionmanager
isoimagewriter

# iPhone photos, and the formats the modern web is made of.
#
# libheif1 is already on the image — pulled in as somebody's dependency — so the
# machine can DECODE a .heic and no application can OPEN one, because the Qt
# image-format plugins that expose it to Gwenview, Dolphin's thumbnails and the
# wallpaper picker live here.
#
# This audience is exactly the one it hurts. A Windows switcher plugs in an
# iPhone, copies the camera roll across, and every photo is a grey placeholder
# with a filename. The library was already paid for; this is the part that makes
# it visible.
#
# Also brings AVIF, JPEG XL, Photoshop PSD, GIMP XCF and a dozen others, which
# is the difference between a file manager that previews what you have and one
# that shrugs at half of it.
#
# THE NAME IS NOT THE OBVIOUS ONE, AND GETTING IT WRONG COST FOUR BUILDS. This
# went in as "kimageformats", which is the upstream KDE project name and not a
# Debian binary package at all, so every build since died on "E: Unable to
# locate package kimageformats" before it produced an ISO. Debian splits it by
# toolkit: kimageformat-plugins is the Qt5 build, kimageformat6-plugins is the
# Qt6 one. Plasma 6 is Qt6, so it is the 6 — the Qt5 package would install
# cleanly and do absolutely nothing for any application on this image.
#
# The evidence originally offered for adding it was circular, which is the part
# worth remembering: "Setting up kimageformats does not appear in any build log"
# was read as proof the plugins were missing. It was proof the NAME was wrong. A
# grep for a string that never existed says nothing about the image.
kimageformat6-plugins

# WEBP AND TIFF OPEN ON PRO AND FAIL ON FREE, WHICH IS THE ONE ASYMMETRY THIS
# PRODUCT IS NOT ALLOWED TO HAVE.
#
# Measured in the two shipped squashfs images: qt6-image-formats-plugins is
# present on Pro and absent on free. It is named in NO package list — Pro gets
# it only as a transitive dependency of one of its creative applications, so
# free lacks it by accident rather than by any decision.
#
# It is a Recommends of gwenview, which --apt-recommends false drops. Same
# family as wpasupplicant and alsa-ucm-conf, but the symptom is a file the owner
# double-clicks: WebP is what a large share of the web now downloads as, and
# TIFF is what their scanner produces. Gwenview, Dolphin's thumbnails and the
# wallpaper picker all fail on both, and the identical file opens on Pro.
#
# "Free must never read as broken, only leaner" — an image format that works in
# the paid edition and errors in the free one is the definition of broken, in
# the edition whose whole job is to convert buyers. Roughly 400 kB.
qt6-image-formats-plugins

# A 2-in-1 with the keyboard folded back cannot log in without this.
#
# qt6-virtualkeyboard-plugin is the on-screen keyboard SDDM and kwin-wayland
# use. Without it a convertible, detachable or tablet reaches the login screen,
# has no way to type a password, and stops there — the owner never sees a
# desktop at all, and no amount of accessibility work later in the session can
# help because the session never starts.
#
# Fourth package tonight found sitting in apt's "Recommended packages:" block
# and never installed: out/free-rebuild.log:2459, inside the block spanning
# 2422-2463, with "Setting up qt6-virtualkeyboard-plugin" nowhere in any log.
# Same cause as wpasupplicant, alsa-ucm-conf and rtkit.
#
# This matters beyond the individual buyer. Classroom and library machines are
# disproportionately touchscreen convertibles, and Pro is aimed at exactly
# those deployments.
qt6-virtualkeyboard-plugin

# THE STICKY NOTE ON THE DESKTOP, AND FOUR OTHER THINGS WINDOWS HAD.
#
# Day two: they right-click the desktop, choose "Add Widgets…", and find only
# the applets plasma-desktop and plasma-workspace ship themselves. No Notes, no
# Color Picker, no Timer, no Quicklaunch, no Dictionary. On Windows 10 there was
# a Sticky Note holding the Wi-Fi password and the school pickup time.
#
# There is no notes GESTURE anywhere else on this image either — Kate wants a
# filename and a save location, which makes a document, not a note. Picking a
# colour off the screen has no tool at all.
#
# NOT a --apt-recommends false casualty, unlike the four packages above: this
# one appears nowhere in any "Recommended packages:" block in the build logs.
# It was simply never asked for. 395k download, 2.2 MB installed.
plasma-widgets-addons

# Brightness for EXTERNAL monitors, which the brightness slider silently does
# not control. Plasma's slider drives laptop backlights through the kernel; a
# desktop with a monitor on HDMI or DisplayPort has the slider do nothing at
# all, or not appear. ddcutil talks DDC/CI over the video cable, which is how
# the monitor's own on-screen menu changes brightness, and Powerdevil uses it
# automatically when it is present. 464k.
#
# Desktops are the machines most likely to be kept and reused by the audience
# this is sold to, and they are the machines where this is broken today.
ddcutil

# BRAILLE DISPLAYS, WHICH THE PRODUCT CURRENTLY DOCUMENTS AS "DOES NOT SUPPORT".
#
# Orca can drive a refreshable braille display and Dagric ships Orca, advertises
# a screen-reader boot entry, and sells into schools and libraries where
# accessibility is a procurement requirement. What it did not ship is the driver
# layer, so a blind owner plugged a display in and it stayed blank. Both
# app-orca.html and site/accessibility.html said so honestly, which was the
# right thing to do about a gap and is not a reason to keep the gap.
#
# THE USUAL OBJECTION DOES NOT APPLY ON DEBIAN. brltty has a reputation for
# stealing USB serial adapters — the Arduino/ESP32 complaint — and that is an
# UBUNTU packaging decision. Checked against the trixie package: the only rules
# files it ships are under /usr/share/doc/brltty/examples/ as documentation.
# `dpkg -L brltty | grep udev` returns one path and it is an example wrapper.
# Nothing is installed into /usr/lib/udev/rules.d at all, so no CH340, FTDI,
# CP210x or PL2303 device is claimed.
#
# 1.5 MB download, 8.8 MB installed, and it turns a published "Does Not
# Support" into a "Supports". The two documents that state the old answer are
# updated in the same commit; they must not be allowed to disagree with the
# image.
brltty

# SCREEN SHARING IN ZOOM, TEAMS, DISCORD AND SLACK SHOWS A BLACK RECTANGLE.
#
# Dagric defaults to Wayland — kwin-x11 above is the rescue session, not the
# normal one. Every one of those conferencing apps runs under XWayland, and an
# XWayland client cannot capture a Wayland screen: the portal picker appears,
# the owner picks a window, the button goes green, and the far end sees black.
# No error, no warning, nothing in any log the owner would read. It fails live,
# in front of a class or a client, which is the worst possible place to find it.
#
# xwaylandvideobridge is KDE's own fix — it feeds the portal's PipeWire stream
# into an X11 window the legacy app can capture. It ships its own
# /etc/xdg/autostart entry, so the package IS the whole fix: no hook, no config.
#
# Another dropped Recommends, of kwin-wayland, in the same apt block that
# already cost this image wpasupplicant, alsa-ucm-conf, rtkit and
# qt6-virtualkeyboard-plugin. 55 kB download, 287 kB installed, and every one of
# its dependencies is already on the image.
xwaylandvideobridge

# THE SCREEN LOCKS AFTER FIVE MINUTES AND THERE IS NO SETTING TO CHANGE IT.
#
# kscreenlocker's default is five minutes and its System Settings page lives in
# a separate package. Without it, Settings has no lock screen section at all —
# so an owner reading a long article, watching a video, or following a recipe
# gets locked out repeatedly and can find nothing to adjust. It reads as the OS
# being broken rather than as a default they are allowed to change.
#
# It is also the page app-accounts.html sends people to when it explains what
# protects a machine that is lost or stolen, so the manual currently points at
# a screen that does not exist.
kde-config-screenlocker

# THUNDERBOLT DOCKS DO NOTHING AT ALL. The classic buy for this audience is an
# ex-corporate 2016-2018 ThinkPad, Latitude or EliteBook sold on with the dock
# it lived on. Plug in the one cable and both monitors stay black, the dock's
# ethernet never appears, its USB ports are dead. These machines ship with the
# BIOS Thunderbolt security level at "User Authorization", so the kernel leaves
# the device unauthorised and waits for userspace to approve it — and with no
# boltd there is no userspace. Windows showed an "Approve this device" dialog;
# Dagric shows nothing whatsoever, so the owner concludes the dock is dead.
#
# plasma-thunderbolt is the approval UI and only appears when bolt is installed;
# bolt without it is a daemon nobody can reach. They ship together or not at
# all. Under 1 MB for the pair.
#
# Sell this as "your dock works once you approve it", never as automatic: on
# pre-2019 hardware there is no IOMMU DMA protection, so boltd deliberately
# will not auto-authorise an unknown device. One click, once per dock, is the
# same gesture Windows asked for.
plasma-thunderbolt

# A MUSIC CD IN THE DRIVE OFFERS NOTHING. The 2013 laptop this OS exists to
# rescue has an optical drive and its owner has a CD shelf. The disc spins up,
# the Device Notifier pops, and there is no "play" and no "rip" — because the
# audiocd:/ KIO worker that Dolphin and Elisa both rely on is a separate
# package. Windows Media Player did this in 2001.
kio-audiocd
